X-Deja-AN: 157184735
sender: news@Orb.Nashua.NH.US
references: <31851EDB.68AD@mtu.cl> <4m7mte$de6@dartvax.dartmouth.edu> <4mqjtf$q3r@corn.cso.niu.edu> <31981144.6B5D@cao.mts.dec.com> <4nft2t$8v6@web.nmti.com>
content-type: text/plain; charset="us-ascii"
organization: Duchy of Wabesylvan Obspauk
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Comments to Peter da Silva's statements enclosed.

>>>>> On 16 May 1996 18:44:45 GMT, peter@nmti.com (Peter da Silva) said:

pjd> In article <31981144.6B5D@cao.mts.dec.com>, Peter Mayne
pjd> <peter.mayne@cao.mts.dec.com> wrote:
>> What parts of C2 don't you want?

pjd> Authentication through files that can't be shared with
pjd> non-digital-unix systems and can't be managed by standard
pjd> freeware tools is the big one for me.

pjd> By the way, the orange book doesn't say shadowed passwords
pjd> are necessary.

pjd> Or ACLs (in fact it explicitly states that user/group/world
pjd> is adequate in 2.2.1.1... they're not required until B3).

Shadow passwords and ACL's are provided to satisfy market requirements, 
not C2 compliance.  Both shadow passwords and ACL's can be selectively 
enabled/disabled.

pjd> The biggest problem with C2, though, is 2.2.2.2. Any system
pjd> that satisfies 2.2.2.2 is inherently undebuggable. For
pjd> software development environments this is a disaster waiting
pjd> to happen.

2.2.2.2 is audit.  Audit and system "debuggability" are completely 
orthogonal.  The above paragraph makes no sense.

pjd> It's also a waste of time as implemented in any system I've
pjd> seen, since without cryptographicly strong checksumming of
pjd> the audit trail (in which case you could simply track access
pjd> to /etc/passwd and snapshot it on close) you have to depend
pjd> on the incompetance of the administrators at penetrating the
pjd> obscurity of the audit logs to create a C2 environment.

pjd> So, in practice, most places trust the administrators and
pjd> run their C2- capable systems at C1 level.

pjd> And by my reading of the orange book, Digital UNIX is
pjd> already C1, and also has all the capabilities of C2 except
pjd> for the requirement that system administrators can't forge
pjd> audit trails... and as I've said that's not actually taken
pjd> advantage of in any C2-class environments.

The requirement for audit trail protection at the C2 level (and through
the A1 level) is that the audit trail be protected from "unauthorized"
access.  This means system administrators, as root, can legitimately
modify the audit trail. 

Note that a skilled root user can bypass any OS security feature. 

Digital UNIX does allow for audit trails to be stored remotely which might
provide some sites with an additional level of assurance (although a
skilled root/authorized user could modify the data while it resided in
kernel memory). 

-- 
Larry R. Scott					IP: scott@Orb.Nashua.NH.US



