|>
|>>In what was does DEC C++ not support IEEE floating point?
|>
|>see the ieee flags on the c89 compiler, expermentation has proven that
|>they do not work with the cxx compiler.

DEC C++ V5.3 on up supports the following IEEE-related switches, 
consistent with the same switches for DEC C on Digital Unix.  To the 
best of my knowledge, there are no outstanding problems in this support.

  -denorm        Force denormalized constant numbers to zero when IEEE sup-
                 port is enabled.

  -fprm option   Specify rounding mode. The following options are supported:

                 c    Round toward 0 (chop).

                 d    Set rounding mode for IEEE floating-point instructions
                      dynamically, as determined from the contents of the
                      floating-point control register. The dynamic rounding
                      mode can be changed or read at execution time by a call
                      to write_rnd(3) or read_rnd(3). If you specify this
                      option, the IEEE floating-point rounding mode defaults
                      to round to nearest.

                 m    Round toward minus infinity.

                 n    Set normal rounding mode (unbiased round to nearest).
                      This is the default.

  -fptm option   Specify trapping mode. Only one option can be used on a com-
                 mand line. The following options are supported:

                 n    Generate instructions that do not trigger floating-
                      point underflow or inexact trapping modes.  Any float-
                      ing point overflow, divide-by-zero, or invalid opera-
                      tion will unconditionally generate a trap. This is the
                      default.

                 u    Generate intructions that trap floating-point under-
                      flow, overflow, divide-by-zero, and invalid operations.

  -ieee          Support all portable features of the IEEE Standard for
                 Binary Floating-Point Arithmetic (ANSI/IEEE Standard 754-
                 1985), including the treatment of denormalized numbers,
                 NaNs, infinitities, and the handling of error cases. This
                 flag also sets the _IEEE_FP macro.



