Now that we replaced our old VAX 8600 (running ULTRIX) with an
AlphaServer 2100 (running OSF V3.2B) some Chem and Math professors who
want to do serious number crunching have surfaced.  They are running
programs that are compute bound for days at a time and our backup
schedule is interfering with them.  We normally take the system down to 
single user mode every Friday night to do a full backup to TLZ07 but I 
am looking for a way to do the dumps without losing these compute bound
background jobs. 

So far, we have attempted dropping down to run level 2 (which kills
the network processes and user logins but retains the background jobs 
and leaves the filesystems fairly quiescent) to run the dumps but we've
found that going back to run level 3 does not bring back all of the
necessary system processes (i.e. sendmail, cron, etc.).  Starting them
manually works, at first, but seems to lead to a subsequent crash over
the weekend ["panic (cpu 0): kernel memory fault"] when nobody is
around. 

If this scheme had worked, I would have only gone down to single user 
mode maybe once a month to get a "perfect" dump.  What am I doing wrong?
Is there a way to make this work?  Any help would be appreciated.  
Thanks in advance.

Jon Leong
Systems Manager
Queens College, NYC

P.S. I know that ADFS has a feature to do a snapshot of a filesystem so
that you can dump the snapshot copy at your leisure but we don't have
the luxury of enough extra disk space to make use of this.



