content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: IKP - Linkoping University
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Talking about that...

It seems to me that I'm not the only one who could imagine him/her
getting for example a Mach64 Pro Turbo or Matrox Millenium capable of
24 bit color depth at 1280*1024 to get more colors or just to keep X
from running out of them. I can take the (relatively small I can say
as I with greaty success have run such card under XFree86 on Linux)
performance hit on redraws and scrolling even if it would be nice to
be able to switch between 8/16/24 bit on the fly.

But some makers of Unix workstations seem to disagree on this as they
obviusly think that the need for more than 256 colors automatically
would imply the need for milloins of 3Dvectors*second^-1 and stuff
like that for which they sell for lots of $$$.

Sun has recently released a entry-level 24-bit graphics card for its
SparcStation 4 which costs almost the same as the 8-bit card. A
full-fledged Mach64 PCI card capable of filling these needs costs
probably less than 1/4th of the cheapest 24 bit card that Digital
offers. So why can't DEC just add some 24- or 16- (more difficult)
support in their X-servers and offer some cheap 24-bit cards?

The Mach64 in the AlphaStation in front of me supports 16-bit color
for Windows NT (Yuck!) but not for Digital Unix.






















