X-Deja-AN: 156639417
sender: root@newsbf02.news.aol.com
organization: America Online, Inc. (1-800-827-6364)
reply-to: huppertz@aol.com (Huppertz)
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


After installing PCNFS on our DECServer 2100 4/233 and PCTCP/ONNET by FTP
on our PC's, I had to realize a strange effect.

The user "nobody" can mount NFS filesystems without using a password from
each and every PC. DEC states that using "<filesystem> -anon=-1" in
/etc/exports would stop this anonymous NFS access.

But that does not work, at least in or environment !

By this, all people with access to a PC workstation that has the
appropriate software installed, will be able to mount all exported
filesystems.
Their access is "only" world-access, but I would rather like to stop it at
all.

With that security hole, NFS is practically unusable under strict safety
regulations.

Any ideas or comments ?



Jochen Huppertz
Kleve / Germany



