At our University we've got a DEC Campus license and one set of media
which moves around between a lot of people. At my department we only
use the Digital Unix related software (Install CD:s, Layered Porducts
and Firmware Updates). Frequently we find that a specific CD is
somewhere else when we need it, which of course causes some
irritation. To get a double Campus media distribution (wich we
obviously don't need) would cost $$$ and just ordering the Digital
Unix and Layered Products disks are also quite expensive.

Now we've got hold of a HP SureStor CD Writer and are thinking that it
would maybe be possible to make copies of the relevant disks. This
thought causes two questions to be answered:

1. How Legal is it? I can't see that it should be illegal, considering
   that we have paid for the licenses but asking can't hurt.

2. Which software can be used. As far as the Layered SW CD:s are
   concerned they have (if I remember right) ISO9660 filesystems and
   could therefore with no big problems be copied using most CD
   mastering software. The install disks (and maybe the firmware
   update CD:s) got funny filesystems (like ufs) which I don't recall
   that your everyday generic CD mastering SW can handle. Can CD:s be
   copied using some type of "raw" mode?

I have access to quite a few different systems, including DUnix,
Solaris, HP-UX, Macs and PCs so if there is any SW for any of these
systems that can solve my problem please let me know.

Thank you for your time

/Erik



