In article <4ntivb$861@coranto.ucs.mun.ca>, tony@engr.mun.ca (Tony Galway) writes:
>We have a DEC 4000/610 and have purchased a second cpu (second hand). Both
>the new and the original cpu work on their own (ie. the system can be
>booted on either properly and work) but together they fail to work.
>
>The diagnostics work in console mode saying that both cpu's are present and
>working properly, however the diagnostic led for the second cpu, on the front
>panel always stays lit (indicating a problem). We can switch the cpu's in
>the slots and it will boot off the other, but the led will stay on.
>
>There is an environment variable in console mode "cpu_enabled" which is
>set to "ff" this looks like a probably place for our problem to be
>located, but we dont know what this variable does, and we dont want to
>check the 256 combinations.
>
>Does anyone have any suggestions? Does this variable have nothing to do
>with it? Is there something really trivial (or even complicated) that
>needs to be done for both cpu's to work? Any help will be appreciated.
>
cpu_enabled is a bit mask to say which cpus in an smp box are enabled. Set 
to FF means all cpus enabled.

>BTW, when the machine boots into unix, it does recognize that the cpu is
>there, but nothing can be run on it.
>

Have you checked that both CPUs are at the same firmware level? They should
be and may give odd results if not as this could mean there is different PAL
code running on each cpu. I don't mean to imply anything but as you bought
it second hand perhaps a check that the second cpu is a 4000/600 CPU and not
a 4000/700 cpu might be useful!

