While I am extremely pleased with GNU's rapid adaptation of the
standardization results into the compiler, I am extremely annoyed that
the g++ front end automatically links the math library, which results
in the namespace conflict (shame, shame!) with "clog" (the GLOBAL DATA
vs GLOBAL FUNC thing in iostream.h vs libm).

1)  Why is this done?  I can't believe that its linking the math
library for convenience; people too lazy to type "-lm" just shouldn't
be using computers.  So I'm thinking that some math code is required at
some stage in the compilation, perhaps by one of the g++ libraries.
I'm curious:  what exactly requires libm?  What is so important that
the namespace conflict hasn't been fixed yet??

