>I guess there are no real hard and fast answers. 
>
>The centralised vs distributed, lots of small vs one large server arguments
>have been rumbling on for ages.
>
>What you need is the right architecture for your organisation, the applications
>it runs and the way it is organised physically.
>
>Centralised gives you one point of change, one management point, one backup
>etc.
>
>Distributed may be better given the constraints of your network putting
>server resources closed to clients and over faster local links may give 
>them big wins. Distributed management software is getting better but it
>is no cheap.
>
>What you probably end up with is a hybrid. Some small one or two large
>servers. What you don't want to do is choose something like NT which 
>restricts the size of you server building blocks so that you cannot have
>that one big machine even if you want it.
>
>What you also don't want is an architecture like NT which has limited 
>administrative scalability forcing you treat all these servers as
>indevidual units rather that one. Granted you can get round this
>by buying something like CA or Tivoli but this is just expensive 
>stiking plaster to hide the cracks underneath which you did not need
>to have in the first place (as a large UK bank has found to their cost)
>
>
>Do buy somthing that is scalable. Both in terms of the size of systems
>that you can deploy (small through to very large). But also choose 
>something that has an administrative and management infrastructure that
>can also scale to manage a lot of users and nodes.
>
