In article <4n1gh0$fs9@pirates.cs.swt.edu>, Wuxu Peng wrote:
>We are trying to back up several Digital AlphaStations
>using rdump.  However,   the server machine has to
>include the client machine names  in its /.rhosts file.
>I somehow feels unconfortable with it.  Are there
>any known security problems with the /.rhosts file?

Yes.

On Digital UNIX 3.2 apparently, you need to be root to make dumps, on
other systems, it is sufficient to be in a group that has read access
to raw disk devices.

You could still do dumps via rsh, as in:

  dump 0f - .... | su - tapeuser -c 'rsh tapehost dd of=/dev/TAPE'

This needs a .rhost file not for root, but for the user "tapeuser" on
machine "tapehost". tapeuser needs no special privilegues except that
it must be able to access the tape device.


