Thanks to everybody for your replies to my posting. My special
thanks to Alan Rollow <alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com> for his detailed
answers to all of my three postings in this group.

This is the solution:

Well, I already mentioned that the ST32550N sometimes got stuck
(not physically but it couldn't be accessed anymore) even under UFS.
But this did not happen so frequently and I could recover from the
situtation by doing a "scu -f /dev/rrz8c reset device". 

I double checked the SCSI bus and I'm sure that the disk has some
sort of fault.

When I tried to copy the disk to another disk, it crashed as well.
The disk light stayed on for about a minute or so and after that
it crashed.

I was able to copy the disk to another disk in spite of that by
opening a second window and as soon as it was hanging, I gave it
an "reset device" command in SCU. This worked! So apparently the
firmware of the disk is too old or it has some other problem.

Well, I managed to get another ST32550N with has a firmware level of
0014 instead of 0013 and this disk works now without a hitch.

Another thing I noticed which may be of some use for somebody:
Badblock remapping is not turned on per default for Seagate disks
(at least for the ST32550N's I got).

You can turn it on with "scu -f /dev/rrz8c change page error-recovery"
and set ARRE as well as AWRE to on.

So thanks again everybody for your replies.

Tom

