In article <31923470.6CF8@edsug.com>, Angela Bao <bao@edsug.com> writes:
|>Hello DUX netters,
|>
|>  We will be getting 50+ Alphastations to run DUX 4.0.
|>All of these nodes will be dataless (i.e. only /, /usr and swap).
|>They should be identical with the exception of the nodename and IP address of course.
|>Hardware configuration is identical also.
|>
|>  I am planning on a way to minimize my efforts to install all of them.   
|>Manually installing from CD one by one is out of the question.  (Especially I need
|>to re-partition the disk to increase /, that is real a pain.)
|>I need a method where I can somehow "clone" a master and 
|>mass produce the rest.
|>
|>  I am curious to know what neat methods/tricks the netters have come up
|>with to install a large site.   Does Digital have a recommended method?
|>
|>  Any pointers are appreciated, and thanks in advance.
|>If there is enough interests and neat ideas, I will post a summary.
|>

Installation cloning is supported in Digital UNIX V4.0.  However, you need to
use RIS (for network installs) to take advantage of this.  If you could set up
RIS on one of your systems - at least for the time when you do your installs - 
you can use cloning.  When you get V4.0, look in both the Installation Guide and
"Sharing Software on a LAN" for more information on cloning.

A current shortcoming - to be fixed in the following release - is the inability
to automatically repartition the disks during a clone install.  However, if you
are adventurous, and you create an "extracted" RIS area, you can actually
modify the installation process such that it could invoke a script which you
provide which could then invoke "disklabel" to modify the partitions in the
way you desire.

Dave

