X-Deja-AN: 156366301
references: <1996May14.170118.1500@xavier.xu.edu>
organization: Georgia Institute of Technology
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.infosystems.www.browsers.x


system@xavier.xu.edu (Carl Dickhaus) writes:

>On an ALPHA running DU 3.2C, on the console, /usr/bin/netscape from
>a Decterm window, as SU, which used to work, now just hangs.
>Sometimes, the only way out is a reboot. Sometimes, you can close out
>the Decterm window from which netscape was launched.

I posted about this problem many weeks ago; so, let me add a few
more details which I learned via testing.

1) Not really part of the problem, but, perhaps worth noting is
   /usr/var/X11/xdm/Xservers was modified to contain the line
   :0 local /usr/bin/X11/X -nice -2 -terminate
   (also, Xdec just loves lots of memory)
2) uname -a produces "OSF1 alpha1 V3.2 214 alpha"
3) I was also able to have remote telnet sessions to the machine
   while testing.  I actually used two workstations living next
   to each other in a lab.
4) I got a copy of 'trace' via "the net" to allow me to trace
   systems call made by the X server while Netscape was running.
5) I could try many different exercises with the end result being
   that the Xserver would finally do nothing but keep calling the
   select function with the same values for arguments until I
   killed the process.  In fact I could reboot the machine, login
   on the console, start a remote telnet session to trace the Xserver,
   start Netscape 2.0 and never do/touch another thing at the
   console until the "select looping" began.

My limited understanding of the problem is that the problem is probably
based upon Netscape using X11R5 and X11R6 being the delivered release of
X11 under OSF/1 3.2.  Perhaps the solution is as one person suggested
to me via email of obtaining older copies of the X11 libraries (a little
difficult for me) - more on this later.

Perhaps the problem was related to the following from 
http://search.netscape.com/eng/mozilla/1.1/faq_navx.html
("NETSCAPE NAVIGATOR FAQ FOR THE X WINDOW SYSTEM"):

  Why does Netscape crash every time I try to copy or paste in one
  of its text fields? 
      On some systems, we linked Netscape against X11R5. If your
      system is not running X11R5, then you need to install the R5
      "nls" directory before any R5 program will be able to copy or
      paste without crashing: this is a bug in R5 itself. 

      When you started Netscape, it should have printed this
      message to stderr:

            netscape: locale `C' not supported. 

            If the $XNLSPATH directory does not contain the proper
            config files, Netscape will crash the first time you try to
            paste into a text field. (This is a bug in the X11R5
            libraries against which this program was linked.)

            Since neither X11R4 nor X11R6 come with these config
            files, we have included them with the Netscape
            distribution. The normal place for these files is
            /usr/lib/X11/nls/ (on SunOS),
            /usr/X386/lib/X11/nls/ (on Linux), or
            /usr/X11/lib/X11/nls/ (on BSDI.) If you can't create
            that directory, you should set the $XNLSPATH
            environment variable to point at the place where you
            installed the files.

      If you install that directory, copy and paste to or from
      Netscape (or any other R5 application) should work fine. 

Is an older version of /usr/lib/X11/nls needed?  Well, the README
file for Netscape 2.0 only mentions that this is needed and in
the distribution for the above, SunOS 4.1, Linux, and BSDI.  With
additional comments below: 

     These distributions also includes a directory called "nls".
     This directory is a standard part of the MIT X11R5 distribution,
     but is not included with X11R4- or X11R6-based systems (such as
     OpenWindows 3.0 or earlier, and XFree86 3.1 or later.)
 
     On these systems, Netscape has been linked against X11R5 (because
     Motif 1.2.4 has some bad bugs in conjunction with with X11R6.)
     Unfortunately, X11R5 has one rather serious bug, which is that if
     this "nls" directory does not exist, the program will dump core
     any time you try to copy or paste to or from a text field!
 
     So, if you don't have the "nls" directory on your system, you will
     need to install it first.  Here is where Netscape looks for it
     (these default pathnames are hardcoded into the executable):
 
             SunOS 4.1:  /usr/lib/X11/nls/
             Linux:      /usr/X386/lib/X11/nls/
             BSDI:       /usr/X11/lib/X11/nls/
 
     If you choose not to create the directory there, then you must
     set the $XNLSPATH environment variable to the directory where you
     did install it.

Does Netscape need to this for all versions of OSF/1 (Digital Unix)
after version 2.0? (Note that I have also crossposted this article
to comp.infosystems.www.browsers.x).  The second paragraph leads me
to think that this problem is probably unrelated to the problem
which I am describing.

