On Tue, 14 May 1996 15:51:16 +1100, Peter Mayne <peter.mayne@cao.mts.dec.com> wrote:
>The encrypted passwords that remain in /etc/passwd are therefore the
>users' previous passwords. Unless you're completely paranoid (and you
>may well be, and I have no argument with that), knowing a user's
>previous password won't get you anywhere.
>So, there's no reason to remove the old encrypted passwords from /etc/passwd.

Not so. It's well known that the old encrypted passwords in /etc/passwd
can be broken with commonly available tools. Hell, that's the whole reason
for shadow passwords in the first place. Having the old encrypted password
around gives a cracker a vital clue as to the new password a user might
have chosen - after all, let's face it: users generally pick lousy
passwords, no matter what rules you try to make them follow.

>What parts of C2 don't you want?

XIsso and XSysAdmin suck large flat black rocks, on top of having names that
are, in and of themselves, offenses to common decency. (StuDlycApS don't
belong in utility names!) They won't even run on my Alphaserver's console!
