kbethke@universe.digex.net (Kenneth Scott Bethke) writes:


>Man I tell you what, I can't unbderstand why people have this idea that you MUST run
>everything on one big overpriced underpowered server.  Your "Usage Characteristics"
>above look like what any ISP would have to face..  They solve the "problem" in many
>different ways, but the *BEST* I think is to use more than one server, use some sort of
>load-balancing, and split up the work on as many servers as it will take.  Terminal
>Servers take the load off a cpu, and you say you will be using them, great, plan your
>whole network like this, have two or three www servers, at least a couple Nameservers,
>mailservers, database servers, news servers, etc, etc.  Don;t listen to the hype about
>Ultra's, the OS isnt there to support the hardware yet, stick to the basics that we all
>know work well..  SS10's, 20's, and yes 690MP's  These are dirt cheap, and with enough
>of them spread around you will have a nice system that will easily take the kind of
>abuse your power-series SGI is taking now...  



I really disagree with this.  I am just about to be moving a Unix network
serving three departments, with over 100 client machines and over 1500 users,
from eight small servers to one big one.  (Its an HP J200 with two CPUs and
512 megs of RAM, if you're curious)

Here's what I see as the benefits to this sort of setup:

1) Easier to manage (obviously)

2) You can afford things for one big server you might not be able to afford
for multiple small servers:  RAID, UPS, lots of RAM, high speed networking,
etc.

3) Reduces cost for networking infrastructure not having to worry about all
these small machines needing to talk at high speeds.

4) Backup performance will be greatly increased since most backups will be
from local disk, not over the network.  If there's an upgrade to DLT or
Exabyte Mammoth in the future from the current 3 Exabyte 8500 jukeboxes, a
local backup is much more likely to realize that speed.

5) Only one OS to worry about for the server -- if you get lots of identical
small servers at the same time, this isn't a concern.  But if you get a few
small servers, then need to increase your capacity in the future and it is
decided to go with a different vendor for the other ones, then you have some
nice interoperability issues.

6) Single point of failure.  Most people think is really bad, but look at how
almost all distributed networks are set up.  /usr/local and other applications
might be on one box, user accounts on a couple others, mail services (and
therefore the mail spool) on another, etc.  This is stupid: if you lose one of
the user account machines, it isn't going to be too long before some clients
are hanging because all the biods are hung trying to access the downed server.
You can make your mounts interruptable, but that's not too smart for data
integrity.  You can also wedge the automounter when it tries to unmount a
filesystem that is gone.  So you end up with a half dozen points of failure
instead of just one, in reality.  And since you can afford a UPS, RAID, etc.
for the one big server (or have a better chance of doing so) you can make the
chances of that single point failing a lot less than one of the distributed
points of failure.


Now if you are talking just about machines for users to login to, then I agree
that having multiple small machines is better in many cases -- I'm talking
about a server, not a user machine.  Putting users on a server isn't the best
idea if you can avoid it, if one of them runs a fork bomb that spinloops, you
can kick the load up pretty high pretty easily, and everything and everyone
else suffers.  I don't believe in allowing users to even put |'s in their
.forward, except for controlled applications, to prevent any chance of a user
accidentally hurting the server.  (Nothing you do can prevent a deliberate
denial of service attack, of course)

But having multiple WWW servers, multiple mail servers??  Very few people
really need this, that's closer to Netscape and AOL territory than it is to
your average sized University or corporation.  Things like nameservers or NIS
servers are different, you have to have backups for these because of the way
the protocols are designed.  But you can make any generic machine an NIS
slave or backup nameserver without much load at all on the system.

In case it isn't obvious by now, I'm a big fan of the "put your eggs into as
few baskets as possible and put all your resources into making sure those
baskets get dropped as rarely as possible" strategy.

