From: murray@pa.dec.com (Hal Murray)
Subject: Re: Selecting interface on dual homed hosts: How?
Date: 1996/05/18
Message-ID: <4njdkh$d7b@src-news.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 155404382
references: <4nhlee$lbb@fstgal00.tu-graz.ac.at>
organization: DEC Systems Research Center
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec


I think you probably want to set things up so that your FDDI and
Ethernet are on different IP subnets.

Then you leave the server listening to any/all addresses.

To run the test over Ether, tell the client to contact the ether address
on the server machine.  To run over FDDI, use the FDDI address.  The
IP routing layer will then send the packets out over the right interface
and the server will send them back the same way.  (The server doesn't
know that the other interface will get to the client.)


For things like this, you probably want separate names in the
DNS database.  I suggest names like Alpha-A-ether and Alpha-A-fddi,
with Alpha-A being an alias for the default.



