Doug Siebert <dsiebert@icaen.uiowa.edu> wrote:
   [...somebody else promotes distributed computing...]
}I really disagree with this.  I am just about to be moving a Unix network
}serving three departments, with over 100 client machines and over 1500 users,
}from eight small servers to one big one.  (Its an HP J200 with two CPUs and
}512 megs of RAM, if you're curious)

   I really disagree with that.  Of course, we're not just some little
   liberal arts school :)  We have ~1000 workstation clients, and
   (excepting file service) ~10,000 PC clients, and over 30,000 users.

   We currently operate (in addition to lots of public lab clients):
     27  Login, "telnet", Servers
     24  File Servers (+8 more coming online to replace 10 older)
      9  Mail Servers
      6  Name Servers
      6  Authentication/Authorization/Accounting Servers
      5  Database Servers
      4  WWW Servers
      8  Misc Servers
      5  Misc Servers
      3  FTP Servers
      2  News Servers
      2  License Servers
      1  Print Server
      1  Workstation Farm

}Here's what I see as the benefits to this sort of setup:
}1) Easier to manage (obviously)

      We have *1* system manager.  And 7 developers who do maybe
      another FTE's work of `system admin junk' between us.

}2) You can afford things for one big server you might not be able to afford
}for multiple small servers:  RAID, UPS, lots of RAM, high speed networking,
}etc.

      We started out with the pile all the services onto as few machines
      as possible model.  It sucked -- all the little interactions were
      horrible.  Putting things on their own box makes problem diagnosis
      much easier and quicker.  It also means if service X needs more
      resources it is a simpler issue to deal with, plus you get a
      nice trickle down effect (the old service X box makes an upgrade
      for less demanding service Y, etc...)

      In our case, it was the distributed model which has brought
      all of those `goodies' (RAID, UPS, networking).  In 1990,
      our campus' idea of high-speed networking was an adapter
      that let you run (at about 1/10 speed) ethernet through the
      serial switch.  Now we have T3,ATM,FDDI,FrameRelay,T1s galore...

}3) Reduces cost for networking infrastructure not having to worry about all
}these small machines needing to talk at high speeds.

      I see the "machine room" as a quite small piece of the
      networking infrastructure.  The off-campus connectivity
      and to-the-desk pieces are much bigger, in my experience.

}4) Backup performance will be greatly increased since most backups will be
}from local disk, not over the network.  If there's an upgrade to DLT or
}Exabyte Mammoth in the future from the current 3 Exabyte 8500 jukeboxes, a
}local backup is much more likely to realize that speed.

      I see this issue as orthogonal to many vs. few servers.
      And I agree backing up from the desktop over the net
      doesn't work to any scale.

}5) Only one OS to worry about for the server -- if you get lots of identical
}small servers at the same time, this isn't a concern.  But if you get a few
}small servers, then need to increase your capacity in the future and it is
}decided to go with a different vendor for the other ones, then you have some
}nice interoperability issues.

      Good thing we didn't go with Apollo, eh?  Actually, we have
      4 flavors at present (DEC MIPS, DEC Alpha, SGI, Sun) and this
      has been more niggling little things rather than any real pains.

}6) Single point of failure.  Most people think is really bad, but look at how
}almost all distributed networks are set up.  /usr/local and other applications
}might be on one box, user accounts on a couple others, mail services (and
}therefore the mail spool) on another, etc.  This is stupid: if you lose one of
}the user account machines, it isn't going to be too long before some clients
}are hanging because all the biods are hung trying to access the downed server.
}You can make your mounts interruptable, but that's not too smart for data
}integrity.  You can also wedge the automounter when it tries to unmount a
}filesystem that is gone.  So you end up with a half dozen points of failure
}instead of just one, in reality.  And since you can afford a UPS, RAID, etc.
}for the one big server (or have a better chance of doing so) you can make the
}chances of that single point failing a lot less than one of the distributed
}points of failure.

      Gack!  If that's how you view a distributed system, no wonder
      you fear it.  This:

pooh.cc> vos move user.janeane fs-10 /vicepf fs-12 /vicepk &
pooh.cc> cat ~janeane/.signature     # still accessible even while in flight
Janeane Hascall
janeane@iastate.edu
pooh.cc>
[1]    Done               vos move user.janeane fs-10 /vicepf fs-12 /vicepk

      is modern distributed computing (fs-10 and fs-12 are not even the
      same architecture).

[...lots of user machines, no users on servers, common sense...]

}But having multiple WWW servers, multiple mail servers??  Very few people
}really need this, that's closer to Netscape and AOL territory than it is to
}your average sized University or corporation.

      If you build it, they will come. 5 years ago 5% of people on campus
      used computers, today 95%.

}In case it isn't obvious by now, I'm a big fan of the "put your eggs into as
}few baskets as possible and put all your resources into making sure those
}baskets get dropped as rarely as possible" strategy.

      I on the other hand, beleive in always mounting a scratch monkey.

John
