X-Deja-AN: 155717298
references: <4nhlee$lbb@fstgal00.tu-graz.ac.at> <4njdkh$d7b@src-news.pa.dec.com>
followup-to: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec
organization: Graz University of Technology, Austria
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec


Thanks everybody for your replies.

In the meanwhile I've learned the following:

1.) For the server its no problem. INADDR_ANY means that it'll accept
    connects from any IP address and thus from both of the two interfaces.
    So nothing needs to be changed.

2.) As for the client side: Hal Murray (murray@pa.dec.com) suggested
    to put FDDI on a different IP subnet.

    But can't this also be done by static route entries?

    Example:

    Alpha A                    Alpha B
    Ethernet IP = a.a.a.a      Ethernet IP = c.c.c.c
    FDDI IP     = b.b.b.b      FDDI IP     = d.d.d.d

    The default interface is the Ethernet Interface.

    When I want Alpha A to connect to Alpha B over FDDI, I do a
    static route entry like:

    route add -host d.d.d.d b.b.b.b -interface

I got this going without troubles. Please note that this is only a
test configuration and I'll tell our customers to better use
a separate subnet for FDDI in the future.

Thanks -- Tom

