In <4npb1h$p7i@sheoak.bendigo.latrobe.edu.au> boc@ironbark.bendigo.latrobe.edu.au (Brian James O'Connor) writes:

>Hello,
>       when I do "ps agux |grep telnetd|wc -l" I get between 
>30-70 processes. Each one is similar to the following (from
>a ps agux)
>
>USER       PID %CPU %MEM   VSZ  RSS TTY      S    STARTED         TIME COMMAND
>root      5915  0.0  0.2 1.43M 256K ??       S    18:00:38     0:03.20 telnetd
>root      5542  0.0  0.2 1.43M 256K ??       S    18:00:20     0:03.43 telnetd
>root      4285  0.0  0.2 1.43M 256K ??       S    17:44:45     0:00.50 telnetd
>root     12487  0.0  0.2 1.43M 256K ??       I    18:15:56     0:00.77 telnetd
>root       287  0.0  0.2 1.43M 256K ??       I    16:59:09     0:00.21 telnetd
>
>
>some are sleeping, some are idle
>
>Is this normal behaviour? 

You should see one telnetd process for every "incoming" telnet session
on that machine, just as you see one telnet process for every "outgoing"
telnet session.

inetd starts a telnetd process for any connection request to the telnet
port (23/tcp).  The process will be alive for the duration of the telnet 
session.

