In article <4ncldq$gr8@newsbf02.news.aol.com>, JPHig <jphig@aol.com> wrote:

>Now, then: Using # dump -0uf /dev/nrmt0h -b32 filesystem1
>                          # dump -0uf /dev/nrmt0h -b32 filesystem2
>                          # dump -0uf /dev/nrmt0h -b32 filesystem3
>I expected to back up onto one TLZ07 tape three filesystems. dump
>proceeds, giving me friendly progress updates as though everything were
>fine. However, after filesystem2, I am told that I have 1160 feet of tape
>left, whereas after filesystem3, I am told that I now have 2250 feet of
>tape, as though it rewound despite my definitely having specified
>/dev/nrmt0h.

>When I issue # restore -t, only the first filesystem is tabulated.
>Issuing # restore -t filesystem2/3, I see "filesystem2/3 not found on the
>tape".

>Any explanations out there? What happened to my backup?

Your commands for writing the dumps are correct.  For restoring
the dump you need to know that the dumps are recorded separately
on the tape.

If you place the tape in the drive and do:

  # restore -i

This will put you into interactive restore mode for the first filesystem.

If you want to restore filesystem2, then after placing the tape
in the drive:

  # mt fsf 1      (or  # mt fsf 2, for filesystem3)
  # restore -i

Read the man pages on the joys of restore.

You also might consider using  vdump and vrestore  if you don't
need to exchange tapes with non-DU systems.  See those manpages, too.

OBTW:  If those error messages that you reported getting were REALLY the
actual messages, then I have no idea what the problem is, but other-wise 
please include specific, exact error messages with your posts.  It helps.


stu
