manager@cse.unl.edu (System Manager) writes:

>Our Central login server is currently an 4cpu, SGI 4D/440, with 196MB RAM.
>we have had to limit the number of simultaneous processes to 600. After the
>number of simultaneous processes hits the high 500s the machine goes
>from being usable to being barely so. The main bottlenecks we feel are
>are memory and I/O. I/O waits are up in the high 30% and swap I/O waits are
>up in the high teens as the processes count creeps up into the high 500s.

I'm inclined to agree with the other reply: it looks like your memory
is too small.  We have two systems in similar usage: computer science
dept timesharing.  They are Sun Sparcstation 10/712's, with 320 MB of
memory.  I don't know the total number of processes we see under load
-- our semester is now over, and not much is happening.  But we've got
671 processes on one of them.  I've got to believe we have double that
during the heavy periods.  Number of processes isn't a number we
typically watch.  We get 100-150 users on each of the machines.  At
100 or just over, it does well.  

At this point I think we have enough memory, but we seem to have
unreasonably long I/O waits on some disks.  We've still got lots of
very old disks, some of them from before fast SCSI.  We're about to do
a major disk upgrade.  

This semester, we had the systems stay up during the entire end of
semester crunch.  (We have 30 days uptime on one and 29 on the other.)
One is running Solaris 2.5; the other 2.5.1.  It looks like Solaris is
finally getting reasonably stable in this kind of usage, except that
we're still seeing problems with stuck and runaway processes
(primarily X applications of various kinds).  We're probably going to
give up trying to fix these problems and just run a process killer of
some sort.  (Netscape is becoming a particular pain.)  

Note that I'm not speaking for the computer center, which has Sparc
1000's with more users per machine.  I'm not sure what their uptime
figures were this semester.  I wouldn't try to scale our numbers to
200 users without talking to someone that's actually doing 200 users.
We've had the feeling that there's a significant difference in
reliability between 100 users on a Sparcstation 10 and 200 users on a
Sparc 1000.  So we've generally preferred to have a couple of SS10's
rather than one big machine.  Not to mention that it's cheaper.  But a
good deal of the difference in our environments is that computer
science majors are an easier population to support than the whole
undergraduate student body.  Our users are less likely to do absurd
things, and it's easier for us to deal with them on an individual
basis if they do.  When you've got 25,000 users and one system
administrator, the world becomes a much more frightening place.





