In article <31981144.6B5D@cao.mts.dec.com>,
Peter Mayne  <peter.mayne@cao.mts.dec.com> wrote:
> What parts of C2 don't you want?

Authentication through files that can't be shared with non-digital-unix
systems and can't be managed by standard freeware tools is the big one
for me.

By the way, the orange book doesn't say shadowed passwords are necessary.

Or ACLs (in fact it explicitly states that user/group/world is adequate
in 2.2.1.1... they're not required until B3).

The biggest problem with C2, though, is 2.2.2.2. Any system that satisfies
2.2.2.2 is inherently undebuggable. For software development environments
this is a disaster waiting to happen.

It's also a waste of time as implemented in any system I've seen, since
without cryptographicly strong checksumming of the audit trail (in which
case you could simply track access to /etc/passwd and snapshot it on
close) you have to depend on the incompetance of the administrators at
penetrating the obscurity of the audit logs to create a C2 environment.

So, in practice, most places trust the administrators and run their C2-
capable systems at C1 level.

And by my reading of the orange book, Digital UNIX is already C1, and also
has all the capabilities of C2 except for the requirement that system
administrators can't forge audit trails... and as I've said that's not
actually taken advantage of in any C2-class environments.

(when I want an unalterable audit trail myself, I log to paper...)

