With <shybnctr78.fsf@lns100.lns.cornell.edu>,
it seems Daniel S. Riley (dsr@lns598.lns.cornell.edu) sez:

[...]
+ [...]
+ pedwards> 	3)  Build a math library which doesn't contain clog(3), and
+ pedwards> 	    name it libm.a; keep the "real" one as liboldm.a or
+ 
+ Won't fix it.  The problem is with libm.so, not libm.a.  If it were
+ libm.a, the linker would just ignore the object module with clog in it,
+ unless clog were actually called somewhere (in which case you have
+ trouble anyway).  It doesn't have that option with shared libraries,
+ hence the message.  To fix it, you either have to change the name of
+ clog in libm.so, or fix g++ to link explicitly to libm.a instead of -lm.

Good point.  IMHO, this might still pop up when compiling statically,
but as you say, they would have trouble anyway.  And I'd love to "fix
g++ to link explicitly ...", but see below.


+ pedwards> 	4)  Find a version of g++ in which "g++" is just a script
+ pedwards> 	    which calls gcc with command-line arguments, and then
+ pedwards> 	    remove "-lm" from the script.  Personally, I think GNU
+ pedwards> 	    made a /big/ mistake when they moved g++ to a separate
+ pedwards> 	    executable:  their goal of "an integrated compiler" is
+ pedwards> 	    now further away, 
+ 
+ I don't understand this remark at all.  g++ is just a front end that
+ calls gcc with the appropriate arguments; whether it is a shell script
+ or a C program makes no difference to how "integrated" the compiler
+ is,

It means I can't get inside the script and "fix g++" now.  It used to
be very easy to change and customize without recompiling the entire
package.  That flexibility is gone now.


+     as long as the C program has the same functionality as the shell
+ script.  The front-ends have very little to do with how "integrated"
+ the compiler is anyway--everything interesting is in the back-ends.

My main beef with the g++ "integration" is...


+ pedwards> 	    and "gcc" doesn't correctly detect a C++ program
+ pedwards>           anymore.
+ 
+ Works for me--the only thing gcc doesn't do is insert the C++
+ libraries and -lm on the link line.  And it must work for you, if g++
+ works, since all g++ does is construct an appropriate invocation of
+ gcc (just like the shell script did).  Compile with 'g++ -v' if you
+ don't believe me.

1)  I never asked GNU to automatically link the math library.  Just
because I'm using C++ instead of C doesn't imply that I require the
hefty math functions present only in libm.  I used to just invoke gcc
-- since the docs specify that it shouldn't matter, that gcc will see a
.C or .cc file and start looking for C++.  Until...

2)  I no longer have my little test bed program which would demonstrate
this, but I finally got fed up one day with gcc giving me braindead
errors on two pieces of reasonable C++ code.  So I bit my tongue and
tried calling it as g++ instead.  The first piece of code turned out to
be just fine -- it was simply a part of C that had been changed in the
C++ specifications, and the compiler was still treating it like illegal
C.  The second piece of code still failed, but it failed with a
completely different error -- which turned out to be the /real/ problem
with that code.

I kept that .cc file for a long time, just to be able to demonstrate
that the testing of file extensions is broken in gcc 2.6.x and later,
and that to insure "C++ mode" one must call it as g++ -- which means
putting up with all of the moronic conflicts in the math library.  One
/used/ to be able to simply use "gcc -x c++ -x none" along with any
other needed options -- much like the script would do -- and achieve a
perfect compilation.  It still does this in 2.7.2, and now the docs
begin to imply that to get a perfect, really-C++-I-mean-it compilation,
one must use g++ because the binary image and only the binary image can
get the options correct.


I'm /this/ close to writing a dammit_i_never_asked_for_libm++
script, which calls g++, grabs the output, and greps out the two
clog-conflict lines, passing the rest onwards.  :-|


Luck++;
Phil

