Martin Hannigan wrote:
> 
> I have a question regarding vdump. In the man pages, it never says that
> I can't backup more than one file system per tape, but it seems like
> that is the reality..
> 
> bkup.sh
> 
> [ relevant portions only ]
> 
> I intitialize some stuff..
> 
> OPTION="-N -D -v -u -f "
> TAPE="/dev/rmt0h"

There's your problem.

> Then I do some day of the week checking..Mon..Fri=level..
> 
> Then I exec vdump:
> 
> /sbin/vdump -$LEVEL $OPTION $TAPE /var
> /sbin/vdump -$LEVEL $OPTION $TAPE /usr/data
> /sbin/vdump -$LEVEL $OPTION $TAPE /usr/data2
> /sbin/vdump -$LEVEL $OPTION $TAPE /home/harp

You're using the rewind tape device. Every time you run vdump, it dumps the 
filesystem, then rewinds the tape, so the next time you run vdump, it overwrites 
what you just did.

Here's a version of a script I use to do single-user mode system backups:

#!/bin/sh
vdump -0f /dev/nrmt0h /
vdump -0f /dev/nrmt0h /usr
vdump -0f /dev/nrmt0h /var
mt rewoffl

Note the use of /dev/nrmt0h (the non-rewind device) rather than /dev/rmt0h. To 
get to files other than the first one, see mt fsf.
-- 
PJDM
Digital Equipment Corporation (Australia), Canberra, ACT
