Dan Pop (danpop@mail.cern.ch) wrote:

: If you use tcsh it makes no sense to bother with aliases.  Simply use
: the tcsh %c feature to put the path in your prompt.  It has the 
: advantage that you can control how much of the path you put into your
: prompt (a full path is usually not necessary and annoyingly long).


You can get around this with a 2-line prompt, which I prefer.  I use
tcsh, and have the following aliases:

alias setprompt  'set prompt="\n%B%n@%m%b:%/\n%% "'
alias cd         "chdir \!*; setprompt"
alias pu         "pushd \!*; setprompt"
alias po         "popd \!*; setprompt"
setprompt

I administer several machines here, and it's helpful to know just where
I'm logged in (and who I am!) so that I don't do something silly (like
an inadvertant 'rm -r' :-)  The bold really helps in this regard.

You can do the same sort of thing in bash fairly easily.  
