What's the problem with removing clog from libg++ when you compile on the DEC
alpha?  I got fed up myself and just changed libg++.  It's a trivial patch to
make; you just have to modify two lines of source code.  (I forget where; just
search through the .h and .c and .cc files for clog.)

If you really need to use clog in libg++, you could add the following line to
the appropriate include file in libg++:

#define clog clog_private

(You might also have to mess around with some of the other #defines, because I
think there's a #undef clog or something like that.)  This would allow you to
use 'clog' in your C++ code, but when you link it would be called clog_private
and would not conflict with clog in libm.
