In article <4mt0vt$4cu@csugrad.cs.vt.edu>, Jeff Aitken <jaitken@vt.edu> wrote:
>Dan Pop (danpop@mail.cern.ch) wrote:
>
>You can get around this with a 2-line prompt, which I prefer.  I use
>tcsh, and have the following aliases:
>
>alias setprompt  'set prompt="\n%B%n@%m%b:%/\n%% "'
>alias cd         "chdir \!*; setprompt"
>alias pu         "pushd \!*; setprompt"
>alias po         "popd \!*; setprompt"
>setprompt
>
>You can do the same sort of thing in bash fairly easily.
>--
>Jeff Aitken

Well gosh, in tcsh, there is a predefined alias, precmd, which gets run
every time just before the prompt comes up.  Thus instead of realiasing
cd, popd, and pushd, just do the following:

alias precmd  'set prompt="\n%B%n@%m%b:%/\n%% "'

That will reset the prompt just before every time the prompt comes up.

