In article <31938467.604@BankersTrust.Com>,
Todd Aven  <Todd.Aven@BankersTrust.Com> wrote:
>We've been using NFS between Digital Unix systems in a
>LAN (10Mb/s) environment for a long time, but have pushed
>back on any requests for NFS in the WAN (until now).  We
>have a situation that will require NFS in the WAN, and I
>would like to prepare the best I can for the onslaught of
>?little? problems.

I hope that you don't mean a traditional WAN because, if you do, you
are dead in the water.  Jump ship and go to one that isn't sinking :-)

The traditional distinction between a WAN and a LAN is precisely that
the former includes multiple administrative domains, though the terms
have been used (or abused) to distinguish slow and unreliable networks
from fast and reliable ones.

The problem of slow and glitchy networking isn't as bad as all that,
though it can be **** exasperating!  After all, most universities live
with such networks, and always have done.  The REAL problems are if you
are running NFS over a seriously insecure network, or mounting NFS
partitions read/write in another administrative domain.


