.\"
.\" The manual page
.\"
.SH NAME
title \- set the title of an xterm
.SH SYNTAX
.NXR "title command"
.B title
[
.I escapestring
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.PN title
command sets an xterm's title and icon strings using
.I escapestring .
Typically, these strings are displayed by the window manager
(e.g., in the wide bar at the top of border).
The
.I escapestring
is scanned for \fB%\fIc\fR escape sequences.
A subset of the
.PN tcsh
shell's prompt escape sequences is recognized.
.PP
If 
.I escapestring
is omitted a default string of
"\fB%#%~ (%n@%m)\fR"
is used.
.PP
The title command can be invoked directly or, more commonly,
through the
.PN tcsh
shell's special
.I cwdcmd
alias.
.SH ESCAPES
.EX
	\fB%%\fR		A single % character.
	\fB%/\fR		Current working directory.
	\fB%~\fR		Current working directory, does \fB~\fR and \fB~\fIuser\fR substitution.
	\fB%c\fR[\fB0\fR][\fIn\fR]	Trailing [\fIn\fR] component(s) of current working directory.
			Include \fB0\fR for a count of elided components.
			Does ~ and ~\fIuser\fR substitution.
	\fB%.\fR[\fB0\fR][\fIn\fR]	Identical to \fB%c\fR[\fB0\fR][\fIn\fR] above.
	\fB%C\fR[\fB0\fR][\fIn\fR]	As above, no \fB~\fR or \fB~\fIuser\fR substitution.
	\fB%#\fR		If root, "\fB#\fR ", else "".
	\fB%n\fR		The username.
	\fB%M\fR		The full hostname.
	\fB%m\fR		The short hostname.
	\fB%l\fR		The line (tty) the user is logged on.
	\fB%t\fR		Current time of day, in 12-hour, am/pm format.
	\fB%@\fR		Identical to \fB%t\fR above.
	\fB%T\fR		Current time of day, in 24-hour format.
	\fB%d\fR		The weekday in <Day> format.
	\fB%D\fR		The day in dd format.
	\fB%w\fR		The month in <Mon> format.
	\fB%W\fR		The month in mm format.
	\fB%y\fR		The year in yy format.
	\fB%Y\fR		The year in yyyy format.
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
	title "this is a title"
	alias cwdcmd 'title "%c04 (%n@%m)"'
	alias telnet 'title \\!:*; /usr/ucb/telnet -ax \\!:*;cd .'
.EX
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.EX
	$PWD		Used for %/ %~ %c %. and %C escapes.
	$USER	Used for %n escape.
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.M xterm 1 ,
.M tcsh 1 ,
.M mwm 1
