In article <4m9s2v$f6u@newton.cc.rl.ac.uk>,
Rachel Kay Street <R.K.Street@rl.ac.uk> wrote:
>2) Also any one tried to mix character*(*) array(length) and char *array[]
>   (or char **array) when making calls from C into Fortran 77 functions ?

I don't know about DEC's Fortran for OSF/1, but that particular mix is
unlikely to work under other Unix'y implementations. The equivalent of
a Fortran CHARACTER array (CHARACTER*(N) array(length)) is usually something
like char array[length][N], or in some cases char array[length*N].
(On machines that are not byte-addressable, I would beware if N is
not a multiple of the word/byte size ratio.)
You then need to pass N as part of the argument list (if calling Fortran)
or retrieve it from the argument list (in a C routine called by Fortran).
The same N applies to all elements of the array.
If you look at the assembly code generated by the compiler for
      INTEGER FUNCTION L(S)
      CHARACTER*(*) S
      L=LEN(S)
      END
you should be able to figure out how the string length is being passed.
Reading the Fortran manual may be easier, if you have it.
Alternatively, you could try
#define N 10
char str[N];
int l_();
size_t ll = N;
printf("%d %d %d %d\n", l_(&ll, str), l_(str, &ll), l_(N, str), l_(str, N));
and study what it prints out (if it doesn't dump core, in which case you'll
have to try the possibilities separately).



