: In article <4m2ndk$l3a@tom.amherst.edu>,
: PAUL D. CHAPIN <pdchapin@unix.amherst.edu> wrote:
: >:   Just curious to know how many of you out there are using the Digital Unix AFS?
: >: Any luck with it?   How does it work with NFS and other Unix platforms?
: >: Any known problems?   Is the community out there FOR it or AGAINST it?

We've had a pretty disastrous time with ADVFS from the performance
standpoint.  It is wonderful to manage and organize your filesystems on
the fly and that was why we picked it at first. We were using it on our
mail server machine which has a HUGE number of small files (400,000
files or so) so it was similar to the news spool sites. ADVFS is
terrible from the performance standpoint in a multiple CPU system (Alpha
2100) under any version of Digital Unix before 4.0. All of the I/O is
funneled thru the primary CPU and your system basically dies.

DEC tells us that this problem is fixed under 4.0. We cannot switch at
this time to verify that. So, if you are running a high I/O intensive
with many small files AND you're running anything other than Unix 4.0,
you will see performance problems. We had to back off to UFS and our
system is still busy but nowhere near the load we saw under ADVFS.

If not, ADVFS should be ok for you. We really liked the management tools
and options ADVFS gave us but we couldn't put up with its lousy
performance under high I/O loads. Again, v4.0 is supposed to have fixed
many of those problem. 


