Won't fix it.  The problem is with libm.so, not libm.a.  If it were
libm.a, the linker would just ignore the object module with clog in it,
unless clog were actually called somewhere (in which case you have
trouble anyway).  It doesn't have that option with shared libraries,
hence the message.  To fix it, you either have to change the name of
clog in libm.so, or fix g++ to link explicitly to libm.a instead of -lm.

pedwards> 	4)  Find a version of g++ in which "g++" is just a script
pedwards> 	    which calls gcc with command-line arguments, and then
pedwards> 	    remove "-lm" from the script.  Personally, I think GNU
pedwards> 	    made a /big/ mistake when they moved g++ to a separate
pedwards> 	    executable:  their goal of "an integrated compiler" is
pedwards> 	    now further away, 

I don't understand this remark at all.  g++ is just a front end that
calls gcc with the appropriate arguments; whether it is a shell script
or a C program makes no difference to how "integrated" the compiler
is, as long as the C program has the same functionality as the shell
script.  The front-ends have very little to do with how "integrated"
the compiler is anyway--everything interesting is in the back-ends.

pedwards> 	    and "gcc" doesn't correctly detect a C++ program
pedwards>           anymore.

Works for me--the only thing gcc doesn't do is insert the C++
libraries and -lm on the link line.  And it must work for you, if g++
works, since all g++ does is construct an appropriate invocation of
gcc (just like the shell script did).  Compile with 'g++ -v' if you
don't believe me.
