In article <4m5o76$dq6@condor.ic.net>, Harold Todd Chapman <htchapma@oakland.edu> writes:
|>How can I randomly access tapes in my TLZ7L?
|>
|>Also, after I use: mt -f /dev/rmt0h offline
|>to get to the last tape, how can I get back to the first tape?
|>
|>In summary: what are all the fancy commands to make my auto loader sit,
|>lie down, roll over, and play backup?
|>
|>Thanks.
|>
|>

I am using a TLZ07 w/a VMS box, so I don't know exactly what commands
you need to give, but on ours, if we dismount and unload a tape, it
automatically loads the next one in the cartridge. 

In order to get it to roll over from the last one to the first one,
though, you need to set a switch on the tape drive. You have to remove
the enclosure, then eject the drawer. You can then see a switchpack
through a cutout hole on the top. Looking at it from the top with
the tape drive facing you, off is to the left, on is to the right.
Number three needs to be on to enable the continuous cycling.

There's a switch you can set for autoloading, too. If it's set, it
will automatically load the first tape when you first insert the
cartridge. If you open the drawer, there's a very small cutout (1/2"?) on 
the top of the drawer that's covered with a small rubber cover. Remove 
the cover, you'll see tiny little switches (there's four). Looking down
on it (with the front of the drive facing you), off is to the left, on 
is to the right. Number 2 needs to be set to on.

As for selecting the exact cartridge, there is no way to do that
without purchasing additional software. In most of our sites, we just
let it cycle through the tapes (we put a cleaning tape in the 12th slot;
it mounts it, cleans the drive, then mounts the first one and continues
with the backups). However, DEC has a software product called MRU
(Media Robot Utility) that costs $200-$400 that you can use to load
specific tapes (ROBOT LOAD SLOT 5, for example) and do backups. We've
been using it as a beta site, but I think it's available to everyone now.

