Hello,

We're experiencing a problem trying to read an in-house produced
cd-rom. The particulars:

The cd contains 31,000+ files on a standard 74 min cd-rom written
using a mac quadra and a Pinnacle RDC 5040. The write software was
Pinnacle's own "CD Burner for Mac".

The files themselves are a combination of unix compressed FITS
files, GIF images, text docs and HTML text files. Every filename
is ISO 9660 standard 8.3 form.

Usually, we 'prep' the cd by creating the files, directories, etc
on a SUN, then I use "Fetch 3.0" to move the whole cd onto a Mac,
then I burn a master cd, then I verify it, then we test it on all
the platforms we have here (Sun, Mac, HP, PC, Alpha, VAX).

The software produced no errors during the write. I subsequently
ran a c-shell script to "cmp" the files on the cd to the originals
still on disk. This compare was successful.

I could read any files on my Mac and on my Sun Sparcstation IPX,
and the HTML successfully accessed these files. I also loaded the
cd onto an Alpha running OSF/1 v3.2 and it also had no problems
accessing files or running the HTML.

Now for the problems:

Loading the cd onto a DEC 3000 station running OSF/1 v3 it crashed
the machine, but only when the USER did it. The peculiarities:

1. if ROOT mounted the cd, it didn't have any problems accessing
the cd and didn't crash the machine.

2. the USER could mount the cd ok, and do something like "ls /cdrom",
but if USER did "cd /cdrom" then "cat README.TXT", the machine crashed.

3. copying files off the cd from somewhere else, like so, worked:

$ cd /temp
$ cp /cdrom/README.TXT .
$

but this would crash the machine:

$ cd /cdrom/docs
$ cp * /local/temp
$

Except this WOULD work if you were ROOT.

I've loaded other cd's from previous master creation runs and this
problem has never occurred before. I wonder if there is a problem
with the number of files we've managed to pack onto the cd. This
cd is using 630MB of the alleged 659MB available with 31000 files.
Our previous runs were slightly under 600MB and with typically
2400 files.

Any sort of insight into what this problem is would be greatly
appreciated.

-- 
cheers,

Brendan Perry



